# What of the user's tree sibyl's commands may read (sibylns(1)): exportfs(4)'s patterns, one
# regular expression a line, matched against paths that start ./ (the tree's root). A path is
# there only if every + line matches it and no - line does. $home/lib/sibyl/patterns and a
# project's sibyl.patterns are read after this file, so each can only take away.
#
# /srv, /mnt, /net, /n, /dev, /proc, /env and /tmp are left: /lib/namespace.sibyl puts other
# things there. For instance, to keep a directory of secrets away,
#	- ^\./usr/[^/]+/lib/secrets(/|$)
#
# sibyl's own sessions, kept in $home/lib/sibyl/n: one session's text is not another's.
- ^\./usr/[^/]+/lib/sibyl/[0-9]+$
